act = {"start": "2026-11-04 18:00", "end": "2026-11-04 19:04", "stage": "Everyman Cinema Screen 4", "act": "Leeds Screendance Competition", "type": "film", "url": "https://www.leedsfilm.com/whats-on/leeds-screendance-competition-5kgn", "blurb": "This film explores Nina's journey from childhood accident and coma into selective mutism, isolation, and eventual recovery. Learning to crawl, walk, run then dance, she adopts movement to speak for her when words fail. Ben Wright | UK | 2026 | 4 mins | English\nA group of young men seek intimacy and human contact in a competitive waltz. An allegory of growing up and the absurd rules we enforce without question, we witness the willing participants contort themselves in their efforts to conform to the pointless, durational game. UK Premiere | Albert Rask | Netherlands | 2025 | 10 mins | No Dialogue\nFour blindfolded dancers perform the twist, impenetrable against change. This experimental dance short proposes a visual representation of hauntology and the persistence of the past. Surreal and uncanny, the film captures the eeriness of a culture that is stuck in a recycled present. World Premiere | Kelia Ideishi | UK | 2026 | 3 mins | English\nLife-Time is an experimental dance film, exploring the theme of ageing. Through the effective use of image layering, we see a woman at three different stages of her life, as she confronts some of the challenges involved in getting older. UK Premiere | Terence White | Ireland | 2026 | 10 mins | No Dialogue\nA magical-realist drama about a young British-Ugandan girl whose classroom show-and-tell unlocks a dreamlike journey into her heritage. The film explores how identity can feel fragile in one space and powerful in another - and how ancestry can become a source of joy rather than something to conceal. Yorkshire Premiere | Ainslie Senfuka | UK | 2025 | 6 mins | English, Ganda\nWhat remains of dance when it is no longer seen? In a single take, we witness a blind woman listening to a live performance unfolding out of frame. Carried by audio description and music, the choreography is encountered through sound and imagination, reframing dance as something not only seen but felt. Yorkshire Premiere | Olivia West Alvarez | UK | 2026 | 4 mins | English\nAbjad Ḥawaz takes its name from the ancient ordering of the Arabic alphabet. Through the body of Salma Zahore, each letter is danced and embodied, reclaiming a language often feared and misunderstood, turning it into a celebration of identity, resilience, and artistic freedom. Yorkshire Premiere | Hadi Moussally | Lebanon | 2025 | 5 mins | Arabic\nAn AI model, tuned to the writings and sensibilities of Maya Deren, collaborates with the filmmaker to imagine new films. Exploring memory, possession, and the digital afterlife, this experimental short questions what remains when the past becomes data and how an artist might lay claim to her absent hero. Yorkshire Premiere | Gabriela Tropia | Brazil | 2025 | 13 mins | English\nA young dancer, crosses the sea, fleeing the shifting winds of a France that’s closing in on itself. There, the sun feels familiar, but the walls speak another language. How does one find a place to call home? Between two shores and an intimate journey through invisible borders lies his deep longing to belong. European Premiere | Pierre-Loys Joubert | France, Morocco | 2025 | 9 mins | Arabic, French"}
act = {"start": "2026-11-04 18:00", "end": "2026-11-04 19:30", "stage": "Leeds City Library Screening Room", "act": "Vera Media: Archiving the City", "type": "film", "url": "https://www.leedsfilm.com/whats-on/vera-media-archiving-the-city-5v2n", "blurb": ""}
act = {"start": "2026-11-04 18:30", "end": "2026-11-04 19:58", "stage": "Hyde Park Picture House, Screen 1", "act": "Leeds Queer Short Film Competition 1", "type": "film", "url": "https://www.leedsfilm.com/whats-on/leeds-queer-short-film-competition-1-r9p7", "blurb": "In the virtual world of The Lounge, you can look however you want - as long as you have your parents’ credit card… A bittersweet hit of nostalgia for every trans person who’s ever played a video game. World Premiere | Heath Virgoe | UK | 2026 | 11 mins | English\nA student returns to rural China to attend their father’s funeral. Amid the gossip at the funeral and the rules of tradition, an unexpected gesture from their mother creates an emotional connection. A tender journey of self-discovery based on the director’s own experience. Yorkshire Premiere | Sun Kun | China | 2025 | 18 mins | Mandarin Chinese\nA short animated documentary that reflects on funerary rites through a queer lens, exploring how the rituals we create to remember those we love should be extensions of their gender identity and the way they lived within their community. Yorkshire Premiere | Mariana Leal | UK | 2025 | 5 mins | English\nA charming love story where Amon, whilst helping his mother run a hotel, feels an immediate connection with the new delivery boy, Roberto. Meanwhile, his best friend Daniella also sets her sights on him. International Premiere | Caio J. Bonfim | Brazil, UK | 2026 | 20 mins | Portuguese\nCumbrian poet Emma McGordon wrestles with mental health and class-based barriers as she tries to turn her poem into a film. Poetic yet gritty, this experimental performance piece weaves together McGordon’s experiences of heartbreak and OCD. World Premiere | Emma McGordon, Chris Neilan | UK | 2026 | 11 mins | English\nJordan puts his child to bed and heads to a party. He meets a past flame, self-assured Atlanta, and they both feel the pull of old feelings returning. Jordan has decisions to make about love, life and taking a leap with someone who lights up his life. Yorkshire Premiere | Harry Brandrick | UK | 2025 | 11 mins | English\nA fledgling drag queen prepares for a performance, changed at the last minute after the panic of seeing a familiar face in the audience. Surreal shenanigans and support from chosen family help put on a show to remember. Yorkshire Premiere | Syra McCarthy, Kyle Casey Chu | USA | 2026 | 12 mins | English"}
