act = {"start": "2026-11-07 08:30", "end": "2026-11-07 10:34", "stage": "Hyde Park Picture House, Screen 1", "act": "Playtime", "type": "film", "url": "https://www.leedsfilm.com/whats-on/playtime-1bvr", "blurb": "Playtime is Jacques Tati’s gloriously inventive satire of modernity, and a portrait of a city in motion.\nAs the bewildered Monsieur Hulot navigates a futuristic Paris of glass, gadgets and labyrinthine offices, he becomes entangled with a group of equally disoriented characters. Almost entirely wordless and packed with meticulously choreographed visual humour, Tati’s expansive widescreen spectacle turns the modern world into an absurd playground."}
act = {"start": "2026-11-07 11:00", "end": "2026-11-07 12:30", "stage": "Vue in the Light, Screen 11", "act": "Germaine Acogny: The Essence of Dance", "type": "film", "url": "https://www.leedsfilm.com/whats-on/germaine-acogny-the-essence-of-dance-nprf", "blurb": "The Essence of Dance is an essential profile of one of Africa’s most influential artists, Germaine Acogny, the ‘mother of African contemporary dance’.\nNow in her 80s, she reflects on a pioneering 50-year career of dance, choreography and teaching, blending traditional West African dance with European influences, and creating the Acogny Technique, which has shaped contemporary dance worldwide. Introduced by Marie-Ange Zié, a programmer at LIFF partner festival Films Femmes Afrique in Senegal."}
act = {"start": "2026-11-07 11:30", "end": "2026-11-07 13:13", "stage": "Everyman Cinema Screen 4", "act": "British Short Film Competition 1", "type": "film", "url": "https://www.leedsfilm.com/whats-on/british-short-film-competition-1-nvsf", "blurb": "A man equally enamoured by the beauty of jazz and his beloved wife, Jim shares his deeply intimate thoughts with us. From New Orleans jazz to the difficulties of grieving someone before they’ve died, Jim is a poetic portrait of a mournful clarinettist. UK Premiere | Stuart Hackshaw, Alistair Little | UK | 2026 | 15 mins | English\nEscaping the chaos of home, a young Jamaican boy finds sanctuary in a community where music, spirit and faith become his guiding lights. But what he discovers there awakens something deep within him, setting his life on an entirely new path. Yorkshire Premiere | Hayden Mclean | UK | 2026 | 14 mins | English\nOlly and Liam’s relationship, like many siblings, is complex and strained. One queer and shy, the other straight and brash, both try to find comfort and make sense of a deeply traumatic event in this reflective drama about masculinity, violence and compassion. Yorkshire Premiere | Matt Chandler | UK | 2025 | 14 mins | English\nA gorgeous animation created by hand painting almost 5,000 frames using oil on glass. Director Oscar Wyndham Lewis wrestles with his own recovery, depicting an artist struggling with alcohol addiction and mental illness, who reaches out to an old friend. Yorkshire Premiere | Oscar Wyndham Lewis | UK | 2026 | 10 mins | English\nBAFTA-winning director (The Black Cop, LIFF 2021) Chez Oteka’s new documentary cleverly explores truth in storytelling, consent, coercion and online abuse, when 17-year-old rapper O.G Niki sings of her experience of sleeping with six men in the same day. Yorkshire Premiere | Cherish 'Chez' Oteka | UK | 2026 | 20 mins | English\nAuthor B.S. Johnson once wrote: “The novel should [...] be Funny, Brutalist, and Short”. Swap novel for film and you’ll very likely get Dug. Short, sharp and stuffed with wit and vigour, it’s a daft, amoral story of drunkenness, coincidence and cul-de-sacs. Melanie Spencer | UK | 2026 | 4 mins | English\nAfter getting a new car, teenager Jamal and his friends are living life in the fast lane! But when late night parties turn into working late shifts, Jamal watches his car change from a symbol of freedom into a relic of his youth, as he’s thrust into adulthood. Yorkshire Premiere | Remi R.M. Moses | UK | 2026 | 11 mins | English\nWhen a drug addict is caught stealing, her night spirals out of control as she’s forced to confront the results of her own deceptions. A naturalistic style alongside professional and street-cast actors lends authenticity to a story of a bleak modern Britain. Mark Chapman | UK | 2026 | 15 mins | English, Panjabi, Polish\nContains scenes of sexism, sexual assult, mental and emotional abuse and suicide and self-harm."}
act = {"start": "2026-11-07 13:30", "end": "2026-11-07 14:49", "stage": "Hyde Park Picture House, Screen 1", "act": "Born in Flames", "type": "film", "url": "https://www.leedsfilm.com/whats-on/born-in-flames-5w8n", "blurb": "A fiercely inventive work of feminist speculative fiction, Lizzie Borden’s Born in Flames imagines a near-future New York where revolution has benefited only the privileged.\nAs groups of women organise resistance, Borden’s kinetic collage of television news, protest and underground radio crackles with punk energy and the possibility of building a new world in the shell of the old. Driven by a thrilling post-punk soundtrack, this exhilarating classic remains defiantly ahead of its time.\nContains scenes of violence, sexual violence, nudity and strong language."}
act = {"start": "2026-11-07 14:00", "end": "2026-11-07 15:45", "stage": "Everyman Cinema Screen 4", "act": "British Short Film Competition 2", "type": "film", "url": "https://www.leedsfilm.com/whats-on/british-short-film-competition-2-5ksn", "blurb": "Feifei, a sweet and adventurous little girl, is dancing between two cultures: as a daughter of a Chinese immigrant and growing up in a white Welsh town. This pensive yet magical short highlights the crossroads many children face when finding their identity. Yorkshire Premiere | Luo Jian | UK | 2025 | 14 mins | English, Mandarin Chinese, Welsh\nAdapting Katherine Mansfield’s 1920s short story, this charming animation humorously updates the tale of two sisters seeking agency and independence after the loss of their over-bearing father. Painterly and playful, the film unpicks our pervasive patriarchy. Yorkshire Premiere | Elizabeth Hobbs | UK | 2026 | 9 mins | English\nWhile documenting his family's history, from fleeing war in Sudan to holidaying in Cairo, Sid Elbadawi begins to dissect his own motives. Reflecting on how we document our history, he questions identity, the complexities of capturing war, black pain and joy. Yorkshire Premiere | Sid Elbadawi | Sudan, UK | 2026 | 15 mins | Arabic, English\nA film about accumulating stuff, running out of time and coming to terms with death. With a sock puppet. i have a was inspired by the illustrations of Richard Hefter and is the latest from Rory Waudby-Tolley, creator of the award-winning Mr Madilla (LIFF 2016). Yorkshire Premiere | Rory Waudby-Tolley | UK | 2025 | 5 mins | English\nA delightfully naughty sex comedy. Young parents Danielle and Harry attempt to reignite the passion in their relationship with role playing dates. Returning home one evening they discover that the babysitter and her boyfriend have been at it on their sofa. Northern Premiere | Tanya Reynolds | UK | 2026 | 15 mins | English\nExpanding on their debut short Ritual (LIFF 2025) and beautifully shot on 16mm, director Cameron Cut has crafted another haunting film of fractured occurrences, as a young man seeks a mysterious driver after a woodland encounter and perhaps, his own obsession. Yorkshire Premiere | Cameron Cut | UK | 2026 | 9 mins | English\n2010: A man walked into a bookies in Dunfermline and placed a £40,000 bet on a horse. He never told a soul, especially not his family. Years later director David Arthur finds the faded betting slip and paints a portrait of his father’s secret vice. Yorkshire Premiere | David Arthur | UK | 2026 | 18 mins | English\nWhen a peaceful shepherd's flock is spared from a mass disappearance that hits a small Welsh farming community, friends suddenly become foe. This eerily surreal folk horror beautifully showcases the reward, but also the price paid for going against the herd. Yorkshire Premiere | Mac Nixon | UK | 2025 | 20 mins | English, Welsh\nContains scenes of suicide and self-harm."}
act = {"start": "2026-11-07 15:30", "end": "2026-11-07 17:08", "stage": "Hyde Park Picture House, Screen 1", "act": "Rehearsals for a Revolution", "type": "film", "url": "https://www.leedsfilm.com/whats-on/rehearsals-for-a-revolution-qk3t", "blurb": "Pegah Ahangarani turns four decades of Iranian resistance into something profoundly personal in this urgent and deeply moving documentary.\nStructured around five people who shaped her life, she traces a history of protest, repression and enduring hopes for change from the 1979 Revolution to the present. Carefully weaving intimate memories with a remarkable wealth of archival footage, this stirring debut asks what it means to keep resisting when revolution remains unfinished.\nContains scenes of violence and themes of suicide, trauma and grief."}
